    {"slug":"knapsack-patterns-boundary-v2","skill":"knapsack-patterns","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":5,"prompt":"Select every distinction that can change a knapsack recurrence.","explanation":"Whether items can repeat and whether the objective counts, maximizes, or tests feasibility changes transitions and iteration order. Input order alone does not define the variant.","options":[{"c":"Whether each item may be chosen more than once","ok":true},{"c":"Whether the objective is maximum value, count, or feasibility","ok":true},{"c":"Whether item descriptions are alphabetically sorted","ok":false,"code":"KNAPSACK_ORDER_DEFINES_VARIANT"},{"c":"Whether the capacity variable is named c or w","ok":false,"code":"KNAPSACK_VARIABLE_NAME_MATTERS"}]},

    {"slug":"linear-search-application-v2","skill":"linear-search","type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","level":"APPLY","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A linear search looks for 8 in [3, 8, 8, 10] and must return the first matching index. When should it stop?","explanation":"It should return immediately at index 1, the first position containing 8.","options":[{"c":"At index 1","ok":true},{"c":"At index 2","ok":false,"code":"LINEAR_SEARCH_RETURNS_LAST_DUPLICATE"},{"c":"After scanning the entire array","ok":false,"code":"LINEAR_SEARCH_IGNORES_EARLY_RETURN"},{"c":"Before checking index 0","ok":false,"code":"LINEAR_SEARCH_SKIPS_START"}]},
    {"slug":"linear-search-boundary-v2","skill":"linear-search","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":3,"prompt":"Select every situation where linear search is a reasonable direct choice.","explanation":"Linear search works without sorting and is often suitable for small or one-pass data. Repeated searches over static data may justify preprocessing or indexing.","options":[{"c":"A small unsorted collection searched once","ok":true},{"c":"A stream examined until the first matching event","ok":true},{"c":"Millions of repeated lookups in unchanged sortable data","ok":false,"code":"LINEAR_SEARCH_IGNORES_REPEATED_COST"},{"c":"A requirement for guaranteed O(log n) lookup","ok":false,"code":"LINEAR_SEARCH_LOGARITHMIC"}]},

    {"slug":"linked-lists-application-v2","skill":"linked-lists","type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","level":"APPLY","difficulty":3,"prompt":"To insert a node after a known singly linked-list node current, which pointer must the new node receive before current.next is replaced?","explanation":"The new node must first point to current's old successor; then current.next can point to the new node without losing the remainder.","options":[{"c":"The old value of current.next","ok":true},{"c":"The list head in every case","ok":false,"code":"LINKED_INSERT_ALWAYS_HEAD"},{"c":"Null in every case","ok":false,"code":"LINKED_INSERT_TRUNCATES_LIST"},{"c":"A pointer to current itself","ok":false,"code":"LINKED_INSERT_SELF_CYCLE"}]},
    {"slug":"linked-lists-boundary-v2","skill":"linked-lists","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":4,"prompt":"Select every operation that is O(1) in a singly linked list when the relevant node reference is already known.","explanation":"Reading the first node and inserting after a known node are constant time. Finding an index or predecessor requires traversal unless separately stored.","options":[{"c":"Read the head value","ok":true},{"c":"Insert after the known node","ok":true},{"c":"Read the element at an arbitrary index","ok":false,"code":"LINKED_LIST_RANDOM_ACCESS"},{"c":"Find the predecessor of an arbitrary known node","ok":false,"code":"SINGLY_LIST_HAS_BACK_POINTER"}]},

    {"slug":"longest-common-subsequence-application-v2","skill":"longest-common-subsequence","type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","level":"APPLY","difficulty":4,"prompt":"For LCS, characters a[i] and b[j] are equal. Which recurrence is used for the state ending at those positions?","explanation":"A matching pair extends an LCS of the preceding prefixes by one.","options":[{"c":"1 plus LCS(i minus 1, j minus 1)","ok":true},{"c":"1 plus LCS(i, j minus 1)","ok":false,"code":"LCS_MATCH_CONSUMES_ONE_STRING"},{"c":"LCS(i minus 1, j) plus LCS(i, j minus 1)","ok":false,"code":"LCS_BRANCHES_SUMMED"},{"c":"Zero regardless of previous prefixes","ok":false,"code":"LCS_MATCH_RESETS"}]},
    {"slug":"longest-common-subsequence-boundary-v2","skill":"longest-common-subsequence","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":5,"prompt":"Select every true statement about longest common subsequence.","explanation":"A subsequence preserves relative order but need not be contiguous, and reconstruction can follow DP choices. It differs from substring and is not generally the intersection of character sets.","options":[{"c":"Selected characters preserve their order in both sequences","ok":true},{"c":"The characters need not be contiguous","ok":true},{"c":"It is the same as longest common substring","ok":false,"code":"LCS_AS_SUBSTRING"},{"c":"Sorting both strings preserves the original LCS problem","ok":false,"code":"LCS_ORDER_DISCARDED"}]},

    {"slug":"memoization-application-v2","skill":"memoization","type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","level":"APPLY","difficulty":4,"prompt":"A recursive function's result depends on position i and remaining budget b. Which cache key is sufficient?","explanation":"Every value that affects future results belongs in the state key; using only i merges different subproblems incorrectly.","options":[{"c":"The pair (i, b)","ok":true},{"c":"Only i","ok":false,"code":"MEMO_INCOMPLETE_KEY"},{"c":"Only the recursion depth","ok":false,"code":"MEMO_DEPTH_AS_STATE"},{"c":"The final answer found so far","ok":false,"code":"MEMO_GLOBAL_ANSWER_AS_KEY"}]},
    {"slug":"memoization-boundary-v2","skill":"memoization","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":5,"prompt":"Select every condition needed for memoization to return correct reusable results.","explanation":"The cache key must represent the complete state and the function result for that state must be stable. Caching does not require breadth-first order or remove all recursion depth.","options":[{"c":"The cache key captures every input affecting the result","ok":true},{"c":"The same state has the same result within the computation","ok":true},{"c":"States must be evaluated in breadth-first order","ok":false,"code":"MEMO_REQUIRES_BFS"},{"c":"Memoization guarantees constant recursion depth","ok":false,"code":"MEMO_REMOVES_STACK_DEPTH"}]},

    {"slug":"minimum-spanning-trees-application-v2","skill":"minimum-spanning-trees","type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","level":"APPLY","difficulty":4,"prompt":"Kruskal's algorithm considers an edge whose endpoints are already connected by selected edges. What should it do?","explanation":"Adding the edge would create a cycle, so Kruskal skips it and considers the next lightest edge.","options":[{"c":"Skip the edge","ok":true},{"c":"Add it because it is currently lightest","ok":false,"code":"MST_IGNORES_CYCLE"},{"c":"Remove every earlier edge","ok":false,"code":"MST_RESTART_ON_CYCLE"},{"c":"Direct the edge from smaller to larger vertex","ok":false,"code":"MST_DIRECTS_EDGE"}]},
    {"slug":"minimum-spanning-trees-boundary-v2","skill":"minimum-spanning-trees","type":"MULTI_SELECT","level":"ANALYZE","difficulty":5,"prompt":"Select every true statement about a minimum spanning tree of a connected weighted undirected graph.","explanation":"It connects all vertices without cycles using n minus 1 edges and minimizes total tree weight. It does not necessarily preserve shortest paths or have a unique solution.","options":[{"c":"It contains n minus 1 edges","ok":true},{"c":"It has no cycle","ok":true},{"c":"It preserves shortest paths between every pair","ok":false,"code":"MST_AS_ALL_PAIRS_SHORTEST_PATH"},{"c":"It is unique even when equal-weight alternatives exist","ok":false,"code":"MST_ALWAYS_UNIQUE"}]},

